                    insight_orientation:    `Service brand restored: you ran the genuine internal-marketing pattern — diagnosed the cause upstream, resourced the internal SLA and treated the front line as internal customers, then allocated the ₱600M behind that constraint with a promise you could actually keep. Satisfaction cleared the 75% target, the service-quality gap closed, and brand equity recovered because the experience finally matched the promise.`,
                    insight_adfix:          `Advertised the fix: budget poured into a campaign that amplified a promise the operation couldn’t keep, so every new customer the ad attracted met the 34-minute reality and left. The ad worked — which is exactly why satisfaction fell further. Share-of-voice without service substance is wasted spend.`,
                    insight_misdiagnosed:   `Treated the symptom: by locating the cause at the counter, in the customers, or in the advertising rather than upstream in internal service, the spend chased the satisfaction score instead of the failure that produced it — and the gap stayed open.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A partial recovery: some of the internal-marketing work landed, but the diagnosis, the budget allocation and the brand promise didn’t fully reinforce each other — enough slack for the gap to persist and for the board to doubt the synergy of the plan.`,
                    insight_overpromise:    `The brand-promise gap stayed open: you advertised a service standard faster than the operation could deliver, manufacturing disappointment with every customer the campaign attracted. A kept lesser promise builds more equity than a broken bold one.`,
                    insight_floor:          `Satisfaction fell below the 65% floor: on this trajectory the board cuts the marketing budget and pivots to pure price competition — abandoning the service-led brand that was Maharlika’s only durable differentiation.`,
                    insight_sla:            `The internal SLA was a target without a system: mandated with no capacity, recognition or culture behind it, so internal departments treated it as one more number to miss rather than a service they wanted to provide.`,
                    insight_gap_open:       `The service-quality gap never fully closed: the experience still trails the expectation, so even a recovering satisfaction score sits on fragile ground — the next disappointment re-opens it.`,
                    insight_brand_lag:      `Satisfaction recovered faster than brand equity: equity rebuilds slowly after the promise has been broken before, so the tracker will trail the satisfaction line for several quarters even on a sound plan.`,
                    insight_overbudget:     `You committed more than the ₱600M cap: the budget is fixed, and over-allocation isn’t a plan the board can fund — discipline on the cap is part of the credibility.`,

                    hint_floor:             `Satisfaction is below the 65% floor — the board is about to pivot to price. Fund the internal root fix and keep only a promise you can deliver, fast.`,
                    hint_overpromise:       `You’re advertising a promise the operation can’t keep — every new customer meets the gap. Re-set the advertised standard to one you can deliver today.`,
                    hint_adfix:             `You’re advertising the fix — share-of-voice is amplifying a service gap, not closing it. Shift budget to internal marketing and a credible promise.`,
